{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghhJZZTQzs1Rcd9v6ZSP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The ground state changes from high spin to low spin at **Dq ≈ 1 958 cm⁻¹** (10Dq ≈ 19 580 cm⁻¹, Δ/B ≈ 21.2). At that point the high-spin ⁴T₁g(F) state and the low-spin ²Eg (t₂g⁶e_g¹) state have the same energy.\n\n**How I got it**\n\n1. I set up the full d⁷ problem: all 120 seven-electron states with the d–d electron repulsion and an octahedral field, solved together. This is the complete calculation behind a Tanabe–Sugano diagram, so the ⁴T₁g(F)–⁴T₁g(P) mixing and the mixing among the several doublet configurations are all included.\n2. I converted B = 922 and C = 4 156 cm⁻¹ into the matching Slater–Condon parameters. C/B is about 4.51.\n3. I checked the free ion at Dq = 0. ⁴P comes out 15B above ⁴F, and ²G comes out 4B + 3C above ⁴F, as they should.\n4. At each Dq I found the lowest quartet and the lowest doublet by computing the total spin S(S+1) of each state. Then I solved numerically for the Dq where their energies are equal.\n5. As a check on which state is lowest:\n   - At Dq = 1 000 cm⁻¹ the ground state is ⁴T₁g (S = 3/2).\n   - At Dq = 2 500 cm⁻¹ the ground state is ²Eg (S = 1/2), with ⁴T₁g about 5 330 cm⁻¹ above it.\n\nThe usual d⁷ Tanabe–Sugano diagram, drawn for C/B ≈ 4.6, puts the crossing at Δ/B ≈ 22. A slightly smaller C/B gives a slightly earlier crossing, so 21.2 is consistent with that.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1958, \"B\": 922, \"C\": 4156, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Full d7 octahedral calculation puts the 4T1g/2Eg crossing at Dq ≈ 1958 cm^-1 (Δ/B ≈ 21.2).\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2625,"cache_read_input_tokens":8058,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2625,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"st